<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhai Mardana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1459">1459</a>–<a href="/years/1534">1534</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Mardana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bhai Mardana was a musician born to a Mirasi Muslim family of Rai Bhoi di Talwandi, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nankana-sahib">Nankana Sahib</a>, and a lifelong companion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a>, the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhism</a>. He played the rabab while Nanak sang his hymns, and became one of Nanak's first disciples. The Miharban Janam Sakhi says he was ten years older than Nanak and his companion from childhood. The janamsakhis portray him as the doubting traveller whose questions draw out the Guru's message. The Puratan Janam Sakhi relates that, when he fell ill at Kartarpur, Nanak told him his body could be disposed of as he pleased. Three of Nanak's shlokas addressed to him are in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Guru Granth Sahib</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhai-mardana.jpg" alt="Bhai Mardana" width="330" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist. Published in sourced book. Page photographed by me (Wikimedia Commons user ThethPunjabi). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail_of_Bhai_Mardana_from_a_Janamsakhi_series_of_painting.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bhai Mardana (1459–1534) was born to a Mirasi Muslim family, a couple named Badra and Lakkho, at Rai Bhoi di Talwandi, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nankana-sahib">Nankana Sahib</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. He was the seventh child, the others having died at birth. A tradition says he first approached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a> for help because so many in his family were dying young. His mother, weeping, told the Guru that she called her son &quot;MarJana&quot;, meaning &quot;he will die&quot;, and when she gave him into Nanak's care, the Guru assured her that people of the clan would no longer die early. The name Mardana is then read as &quot;does not die&quot; in Punjabi.</p><p>The Miharban Janam Sakhi says he was ten years older than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Nanak</a> and had been his companion since childhood, and that he sang hymns by Kabir, Trilochan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravidas">Ravidas</a>, Dhanna and Beni. According to Ratan Singh Bhangu's Prachin Panth Prakash, the boy Nanak gave him a stringed instrument made of reeds. When Nanak took charge of the granaries and stores of the Nawab of Sultanpur Lodhi, Mardana, by then married with two sons and a daughter, went to see him on behalf of Nanak's father, and never returned home.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Mardana played the rabab, or rebeck, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Nanak</a> sang his words about God, and became one of his first disciples. Before the long journeys Mardana wished to marry off his daughter, and the disciple Bhai Baghirath helped him materially so that he could go. The chronicles of their travels, among them the Puratan Janam Sakhi, use Mardana to show worldly doubts and to draw out Nanak's message, portraying him as doubtful and asking for clarification in every situation.</p><p>Three shlokas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a> addressed to Mardana, against the use of wine, are in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Guru Granth Sahib</a>. A composition of Mardana's own appears there in Bihagadre ki Var, along with two of Nanak's addressed to him, and it holds that an evil body may be cleansed of sin in the sangat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Puratan Janam Sakhi tells that at Kartarpur Mardana, old and worn by his travels, fell ill. Since he had been born a Muslim and was now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a>, the question arose how his body should be treated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Nanak</a> listed the customs of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> and told him it should be disposed of as he pleased. Nanak had his body given to the Ravi River, the Sohila read and karah parshad distributed. Nanak then asked Mardana's son Shahzada to take his father's place, and Shahzada accompanied him until his death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bhai Mardana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
